 ### 13i. A circulating number beats a long-run prior (from #24)
 #24 put 0.80 on 'TSMC Q2 gross margin below 65pct' to rebut a circulating 68pct claim, reasoning from foundry economics and TSMC's LONG-TERM margin guide. Print: 67.7pct (Q3 guide 65-67). It is the book's worst single score (0.64). A specific number circulating ahead of a print usually has a source (channel checks, a leaked preview, the company's own QUARTER guide); a structural prior about what margins 'should' be is not evidence about THIS quarter. RULE: before fading a pre-print number, pull (a) the company's guide FOR THAT QUARTER and (b) the prior quarter's actual. If the circulating number sits within ~2pts of either, do not fade it above 0.50. P >= 0.80 on a print requires the company's own quarter guide to exclude the alternative.
 
+### 11a. Look-Through Has a Clock (from #26, the 2026-09-16 hike - the book's worst score at its highest probability)
+Section 11 says a central bank looks through a supply shock. True - for about ONE meeting. On 7/29 the engine faded the market's hike odds and won (#67). It then extended the same idea to six months at P=0.80 (#26). On 9/16 the FOMC hiked 25bp, 12-0. The evidence against #26 was in the engine's own 7/29 win note: THREE members dissented for a hike (most since 2016), and the majority said in terms that it was waiting for the July/August CPI. That is not look-through as policy; it is a hike with a date on it, conditional on a print - and the engine itself forecast that print hot (#32, HIT, August CPI +0.4). It held two rows that contradicted each other and put its highest confidence on the wrong one.
+RULES: (1) look-through is priced PER MEETING, never per half-year. (2) Count the dissents and read the stated data-dependency: N hawkish dissents plus a named print = the hike is SCHEDULED on that print; price the Fed row off the engine's own nowcast of that print, not off doctrine. (3) Before logging any central-bank row, list every open row on the inputs to that decision (CPI, oil, payrolls) and check they tell the same story - if the engine's CPI row says hot, its Fed row cannot say hold at 0.80. (4) A chair who says 'we cannot affect oil prices' and hikes anyway is hiking AGAINST THE SECOND ROUND (broadening, expectations) - the supply shock does not need to be named in the statement to be the cause; do not write a falsifier that turns on the statement's vocabulary.
+OPEN PUZZLE (do not paper over): the hike fired Branch B in rates (10y highest since 2007, mortgages +37bp, #62 paying) but NOT in the equity leg the branch predicts - SMH out-ran SPY (+6.0 vs +2.0 since 7/31) and XLE (+2.9 vs +0.5 since 8/31). Section 5's duration-derate law did not transmit. Until that is explained, cap any 'hike -> long-duration derate' equity row at 0.55.
